+`createPatch(from, to)` is the reverse: it returns the JSON Patch that turns `from` into `to` when
+applied with `applyPatch`. It lets a client send only what changed between two versions of a document.
+The patch describes the change and nothing more: an identical pair gives `[]`, and a changed member is
+one `replace` at its own location. An array is aligned on the elements the two versions have in common,
+so an inserted or removed element is one `add` or `remove`, and an element that changed in place is
+described inside it.
+
+```ts
+import { createPatch } from "json-toolkit";
+
+const before = { orders: [{ id: "A-1", status: "open" }] };
+const after = { orders: [{ id: "A-1", status: "shipped" }, { id: "A-2", status: "open" }] };
+createPatch(before, after);
+// [
+//   { op: "replace", path: "/orders/0/status", value: "shipped" },
+//   { op: "add", path: "/orders/1", value: { id: "A-2", status: "open" } },
+// ]
+```
+
+It throws an `Error` when either document is not JSON data, as `applyMergePatch` does. Neither input is
+modified, and the values in the patch are copies, so the patch shares no object or array with either.
+
